> I am using measurementInvarianceCat here, but my model is not giving any information and have been trying to yield output for last four hours.

Have you tried just fitting your configural model with cfa() to see how long estimation takes?  You have 35 categorical indicators (how many categories?), so this is a pretty big model that could take a while to estimate, although 4 hours does seem like a long time to wait.  If estimation of the configural model is prohibitively long

> Please let me know if there is some trouble in my model specification. My model is a second order CFA with one factor at second level and five factors at first order level.

Well, your syntax looks fine, but the syntax only specifies the first-order measurement model.  Perhaps you realize this already, but I bring it up because measurementInvarianceCat() can only handle first-order measurement models.  There is a more general syntax-writing function that can facilitate testing invariance of a second-order measurement model:

https://groups.google.com/d/msg/lavaan/oKwP0_6-i1g/i0jGCU-LBwAJ

It can also test invariance across both groups and occasions, which seems applicable here if I correctly interpret the factor names (t1 - t5) as indicating 5 occasions.  If you use the syntax-writing function, I would still recommend fitting one model at a time.  The automated fitting of a series of models prevents knowing when problems occur, either with estimation or whether it makes sense to test strong invariance if weak invariance is rejected.

