MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> The data of these indicators is dichotomous.

In that case, you cannot separately test loadings and thresholds.

https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11336-016-9506-0

So you need to compare your configural model directly to a model with both loadings and thresholds constrained to equality.

> fitmeasures(fit.t2, fit.measures = c("chisq", "df", "pvalue", "CFI", "RMSEA", "TLI"))
>
>
>
> these are the deduced values of fit-indices:
>
> #chisq     df pvalue    cfi  rmsea    tli
>
> #31.105 24.000  0.151  0.935  0.018  0.886
>
>
>
> Based on this, I concluded that my configural model fits well for my two groups.

You are looking at the wrong indices.  You have categorical data, which require a robust test statistic, so you should look at

 fit.measures = c("chisq.scaled", "df.scaled", "pvalue.scaled",
                  "cfi.scaled", "rmsea.scaled", "tli.scaled")

> And I got my fit indices as:
>
> chisq     df  pvalue    cfi  rmsea    tli
>
> 38.319 29.000  0.115   0.915  0.019  0.877
>
>
>
>
>
> Based on this, I concluded that scalar model holds good for two groups.

You cannot conclude that by looking at the fit of the model alone (even when you look at the correct fit measures).  You need to statistically compare the fit of this model to the configural model, using a likelihood-ratio test (often called a chi-squared difference test):

lavTestLRT(fit.t2, fit.t2.s)

> Now, I am unsure if I can check the STRICT INVARIANCE or not, as I have read that for BINARY data we don’t work on residual variance.

You can, because you set parameterization = "theta".  After constraining loadings and thresholds to equality across groups, the residual variances can be estimated in all but the first group (where they are still fixed to 1 for identification).  To test them for equality, you can constrain them all to 1 again and compare that model to the strong-invariance model.  But strict invariance is not necessary for comparing latent parameters, so perhaps this step is not needed, depending on what your next step after testing invariance will be.

