> INSTEAD OF FIXING THE RESIDUAL VARIANCE, CAN I FIX THE LATENT VARIABLE VARIANCE HERE as I am not interested in studying the residual variance

There are 2 types of latent variable:
common factors (i.e., measured by multiple indicators, such as your factor t2)
latent item-responses (i.e., the normally distributed "propensity" for responding 1 instead of 0 to each observed binary indicator)
If you are interested in constraining common-factor variances (or covariances between common factors), you can validly test their equivalence without assuming residual variances are equivalent.  Likewise, if you want to compare common-factor means across groups or occasions, you do not need to constrain residual variances.

> lavTestLRT(fit.t2scalar, fit.t2v)
>
> Scaled Chi Square Difference Test (method = "satorra.2000")
>
>
>
>         Df AIC BIC  Chisq Chisq diff Df diff Pr(>Chisq)
>
> fit.t2scalar 27         32.246
>
> fit.t2v 28         32.649    0.37175       1     0.5421
>
> Based on this can I say that we have invariant thresholds, loadings and Latent variable variances.

Yes, common-factor variances are equivalent (check your summary() output to see which parameters are held equal).

> 1.       I am just confused due to dichotomous data. I have read in Random Permutation Testing Applied to Measurement Invariance Testing with Ordered Categorical Indicators by Benjamin A. Kite, Terrence D. Jorgensen & Po-Yi Chen that “fixing residual variances of latent item-responses to 1 ^λ2, so that the total variance of each latent item-response equals one” (p.580) . Do I need to do that from the first configural model. Is it required? Or the specification done in the model is okay?

See my note above about latent item-responses.  That is not what you constrained above.  Constraining the total variance of latent item-responses requires setting parameterization = "delta" and setting the scale parameters (see ~*~ operators in your summary() output).  You can read details here:

Kamata, A., & Bauer, D. J. (2008). A note on the relation between factor analytic and item response theory models. Structural Equation Modeling, 15(1), 136-153. https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/10705510701758406

https://www.statmodel.com/download/webnotes/CatMGLong.pdf

>  2.       Also, I am freely estimating my latent variable variance in configural and scalar model, because fixing them to 1 gave me real poor fit-indices:

You identify the scale of the common factor by fixing the first factor loading to 1, so you need to estimate the common-factor variance.

> 3.       Some of my models have failed to show configural invariance, is it good idea to switch to IRT and study the DIF pattern for the items related to that specific latent construct?

You can, but with binary items, CFA is equivalent to a 2-parameter normal-ogive IRT model. Unless you add a guessing parameter (a 3-parameter IRT model), then I don't know if there is a benefit to moving to IRT.

